//! Caravan objects -- the resource kinds a verified kernel offers, held beside
//! the resource Caravan's own supervision names today.
//!
//! WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS. `capabilities.rye` names a resource the simplest way
//! a hosted table can: a **string and a rights mask**. That was whole while the
//! table answered only to itself. A root task on seL4 hands out **typed
//! objects**, and on riscv64 there are **nine** of them -- a count read out of
//! the compiled enums on `20260821.043831`, never recited.
//!
//! So this module does one thing, completely: it states the nine in their own
//! numbering, names the tier each belongs to, says in plain words what each is
//! for, and names which Caravan module stands for it today or which subsystem
//! owes one. The reading is policy -- pure Rye, hosted, no kernel beneath it --
//! and it is asserted rather than described, so the compiler and a witness both
//! hold it.
//!
//! THE FINDING, WHICH THE ACCOUNTING FOUND RATHER THAN SOUGHT. Caravan already
//! stands for **five** of the nine, and the five are **exactly the portable
//! tier**: `untyped.rye` carves memory with a real floor, `channels.rye` rosters
//! the protection domains a run schedules, `reply.rye` reads an ask through four
//! stages and answers it, `notify.rye` rings a declared channel as its own
//! doorbell, and `capabilities.rye` keeps the table of who may do what. The
//! **four** it owes are exactly the mode and architecture tiers -- the mapping
//! objects that build an address space. A hosted supervision tree, grown one
//! bounded rung at a time and aimed at nothing in particular, arrived at the
//! architecture-independent core of a formally verified kernel and stopped
//! precisely where the architecture begins. That alignment is asserted below as
//! a single invariant rather than admired in prose.
//!
//! THE THREE TIERS, AND WHY THEY COMPOSE. seL4 declares object types in three
//! enums: a portable one, a mode one that **begins** at the portable one's
//! sentinel, and an architecture one that begins at the mode one's sentinel.
//! The three therefore compose into a single numbering with no overlap and no
//! gap -- the compose-not-braid shape this tree reaches for, arrived at
//! independently by a formally verified kernel. The composition is asserted
//! below rather than admired, and the witness reads the anchoring out of the
//! headers themselves.
//!
//! WHAT `regions.rye` DOES AND DOES NOT COVER. It declares **which** memory a
//! dependent may touch and how far, which is the policy half. The four mapping
//! objects are the mechanism that makes such a declaration true on a kernel --
//! page sizes chosen and page-table levels built by the supervisor. Caravan has
//! never had to build one, so the debt is real and named rather than waved at.
//!
//! WHICH CONFIGURATION. Nine is the count for the configuration this arc brings
//! up first: **non-MCS**, three page-table levels. seL4 declares three further
//! object types behind configuration guards -- a scheduling context and a reply
//! object under `CONFIG_KERNEL_MCS`, and a tera page above three levels. They
//! are real seL4 and absent here, so the witness counts them rather than
//! passing over them, and a configuration flip reds this module on the lap it
//! lands.
//!
//! TWO ROOMS. The nine names, their order, and the tier anchoring are
//! checkable, and `tools/caravan_objects_witness.rish` binds them against
//! seL4's own BSD-2-Clause headers on metal. Which Caravan concept *should*
//! stand on each object remains a design judgement, named as one below, earning
//! its proof the day an object is actually retyped.
//!
//! CLEAN ROOM. No seL4 line is reproduced here. The identifiers below are
//! interface names, written so a later binding can be diffed against the
//! headers we are licensed to include.
//!
//! Usage:
//!   objects          Run the self-test (default)
//!   objects selftest Same as default
//!   objects table    Print the nine, their symbols, tiers, and who stands for each

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const caravan_objects_version = "20260821.220352";

/// How many object types a riscv64 seL4 offers in the configuration this arc
/// brings up first, seL4's `seL4_ObjectTypeCount`. Read from the compiled enum
/// `20260821.043831`; the witness re-reads the symbols from the vendored
/// headers on every run, so a vendored bump that moves the vocabulary reds this
/// module on the lap it lands.
pub const kernel_object_count: u32 = 9;

/// Where the portable tier ends and the mode tier begins, seL4's
/// `seL4_NonArchObjectTypeCount`.
pub const non_arch_object_type_count: u32 = 5;

/// Where the mode tier ends and the architecture tier begins, seL4's
/// `seL4_ModeObjectTypeCount` on riscv64 with three page-table levels.
pub const mode_object_type_count: u32 = 6;

/// How many further object types stand behind a configuration guard: a
/// scheduling context and a reply object under MCS, and a tera page above three
/// page-table levels. Named so their absence is a measured fact rather than an
/// oversight.
pub const configuration_guarded_count: u32 = 3;

// =========================================================================
// The nine -- what a kernel hands out
// =========================================================================

/// One kind of object a capability may name. Value order matches the interface
/// numbering across all three tiers, so a later binding may cast rather than
/// translate.
pub const KernelObject = enum(u8) {
    untyped = 0,
    tcb = 1,
    endpoint = 2,
    notification = 3,
    cap_table = 4,
    giga_page = 5,
    four_k_page = 6,
    mega_page = 7,
    page_table = 8,
};

/// The interface symbol each object carries, so a witness can diff this module
/// against the headers rather than against a memory of them.
pub fn kernel_symbol(object: KernelObject) []const u8 {
    // invariant: every arm returns a non-empty symbol -- the switch is total.
    const symbol: []const u8 = switch (object) {
        .untyped => "seL4_UntypedObject",
        .tcb => "seL4_TCBObject",
        .endpoint => "seL4_EndpointObject",
        .notification => "seL4_NotificationObject",
        .cap_table => "seL4_CapTableObject",
        .giga_page => "seL4_RISCV_Giga_Page",
        .four_k_page => "seL4_RISCV_4K_Page",
        .mega_page => "seL4_RISCV_Mega_Page",
        .page_table => "seL4_RISCV_PageTableObject",
    };
    assert(symbol.len > 0); // invariant: a named object always carries its name
    return symbol;
}

/// Plain English for the object, in Caravan's own words.
pub fn kernel_meaning(object: KernelObject) []const u8 {
    const meaning: []const u8 = switch (object) {
        .untyped => "a region of memory holding no type yet, from which every other object is carved",
        .tcb => "one thread the kernel schedules, with its registers and its priority",
        .endpoint => "a meeting point where one thread's call waits for another thread's answer",
        .notification => "a signal one thread raises and another waits on, carrying no message",
        .cap_table => "a table of slots, each holding one capability, forming a thread's authority",
        .giga_page => "a gigabyte of memory mapped as one page",
        .four_k_page => "four kilobytes of memory mapped as one page",
        .mega_page => "two megabytes of memory mapped as one page",
        .page_table => "one level of the tree that turns a virtual address into a real one",
    };
    assert(meaning.len > 0); // invariant: every object is legible
    return meaning;
}

// =========================================================================
// The three tiers, and the numbering they compose into
// =========================================================================

/// Which enum an object is declared in. The tiers are a fact about seL4's
/// headers, and they matter here because the numbering only holds if each tier
/// begins exactly where the last one ended.
pub const Tier = enum(u8) {
    portable = 0,
    mode = 1,
    architecture = 2,
};

/// The tier an object belongs to, derived from its own number rather than
/// listed, so the tier and the numbering can never disagree.
pub fn tier_of(object: KernelObject) Tier {
    const value: u32 = @intFromEnum(object);
    assert(value < kernel_object_count); // invariant: every object sits inside the vocabulary
    if (value < non_arch_object_type_count) return .portable;
    if (value < mode_object_type_count) return .mode;
    return .architecture;
}

/// How many objects a tier holds.
pub fn tier_width(tier: Tier) u32 {
    const width: u32 = switch (tier) {
        .portable => non_arch_object_type_count,
        .mode => mode_object_type_count - non_arch_object_type_count,
        .architecture => kernel_object_count - mode_object_type_count,
    };
    assert(width >= 1); // invariant: a declared tier holds at least one object
    return width;
}

// =========================================================================
// What Caravan stands for today, and what it owes
// =========================================================================

/// Whether a Caravan module stands for an object today, or which subsystem owes
/// one. `held` marks the five already spoken for.
pub const Standing = enum(u8) {
    held = 0,
    the_address_space = 1,
};

/// Which Caravan concept stands for each object, or which subsystem owes one.
///
/// DESIGN JUDGEMENT, named as one. Each of the five holdings below is a module
/// on disk read this round, never a memory of one -- an earlier draft of this
/// file claimed Caravan owed untyped memory and the signal, and both had
/// already been built. The four mapping objects are one debt wearing four
/// sizes, and it stands.
pub fn standing_of(object: KernelObject) Standing {
    // invariant: the switch is total over the nine.
    const standing: Standing = switch (object) {
        .untyped, .tcb, .endpoint, .notification, .cap_table => .held,
        .giga_page, .four_k_page, .mega_page, .page_table => .the_address_space,
    };
    // invariant: the holdings are exactly the portable tier -- the finding, asserted.
    assert((standing == .held) == (tier_of(object) == .portable));
    return standing;
}

/// One line naming what stands for the object, or why the owing subsystem owes
/// it, so the agenda reads as design rather than as a list.
pub fn standing_reason(object: KernelObject) []const u8 {
    const reason: []const u8 = switch (object) {
        .untyped => "untyped.rye carves memory with a real floor, measured after alignment",
        .tcb => "channels.rye rosters the protection domains, and concurrent.rye runs them as real processes",
        .endpoint => "reply.rye reads one ask through four stages and answers it",
        .notification => "notify.rye provisions each declared channel as its own doorbell",
        .cap_table => "capabilities.rye keeps a bounded table of dependents and their rights",
        .giga_page, .four_k_page, .mega_page, .page_table => "regions.rye declares the memory, mapping.rye plans the pages exactly; the retype itself wants a kernel",
    };
    assert(reason.len > 0); // invariant: every standing names its reason
    return reason;
}

// =========================================================================
// Counting the agenda
// =========================================================================

/// The nine, in interface order. One list, so no count is ever recited.
pub const all_kernel_objects = [_]KernelObject{
    .untyped,     .tcb,         .endpoint,
    .notification, .cap_table,  .giga_page,
    .four_k_page, .mega_page,   .page_table,
};

/// How many of the nine Caravan already stands for. Counted from the standing
/// of each object rather than recited, so the number and the table agree.
pub fn held_count() u32 {
    var count: u32 = 0;
    for (all_kernel_objects) |object| {
        if (standing_of(object) == .held) count += 1;
    }
    assert(count <= kernel_object_count); // invariant: holdings never exceed the vocabulary
    return count;
}

/// How many of the nine remain as design debt.
pub fn owed_count() u32 {
    const held = held_count();
    assert(held <= kernel_object_count); // invariant: the subtraction stays in range
    const remaining = kernel_object_count - held;
    assert(held + remaining == kernel_object_count); // invariant: the two partition the vocabulary
    return remaining;
}

// =========================================================================
// The table, printed
// =========================================================================

fn print_table() u8 {
    print("objects: version {s}\n", .{caravan_objects_version});
    for (all_kernel_objects) |object| {
        print("objects: {d:>2} {s} [{s}] -- {s} [{s}] {s}\n", .{
            @intFromEnum(object),
            kernel_symbol(object),
            @tagName(tier_of(object)),
            kernel_meaning(object),
            @tagName(standing_of(object)),
            standing_reason(object),
        });
    }
    return 0;
}

// =========================================================================
// Self-test -- assert the numbering composes, the tiers partition, and the
// agenda is honestly counted
// =========================================================================

fn run_self_test() u8 {
    // The list and the enum agree, so the count is read rather than recited.
    assert(all_kernel_objects.len == kernel_object_count);
    var seen: u32 = 0;
    for (all_kernel_objects, 0..) |object, index| {
        assert(@intFromEnum(object) == index);
        seen += 1;
    }
    assert(seen == kernel_object_count);

    // The three tiers compose into one numbering with no overlap and no gap.
    assert(non_arch_object_type_count < mode_object_type_count);
    assert(mode_object_type_count < kernel_object_count);
    assert(tier_width(.portable) + tier_width(.mode) + tier_width(.architecture) == kernel_object_count);
    assert(tier_width(.portable) == 5);
    assert(tier_width(.mode) == 1);
    assert(tier_width(.architecture) == 3);

    // Every object lands in exactly the tier its number places it in.
    var portable_seen: u32 = 0;
    var mode_seen: u32 = 0;
    var arch_seen: u32 = 0;
    for (all_kernel_objects) |object| {
        switch (tier_of(object)) {
            .portable => portable_seen += 1,
            .mode => mode_seen += 1,
            .architecture => arch_seen += 1,
        }
    }
    assert(portable_seen == tier_width(.portable));
    assert(mode_seen == tier_width(.mode));
    assert(arch_seen == tier_width(.architecture));

    // The portable core is the five a supervised root task may count on
    // anywhere, and the four that remain belong to riscv64.
    assert(tier_of(.untyped) == .portable);
    assert(tier_of(.cap_table) == .portable);
    assert(tier_of(.giga_page) == .mode);
    assert(tier_of(.page_table) == .architecture);

    // The agenda partitions the vocabulary: five held, four owed.
    const held = held_count();
    const owed = owed_count();
    assert(held == 5);
    assert(owed == 4);
    assert(held + owed == kernel_object_count);

    // Every object names a real standing, and the holdings line up with the tier.
    for (all_kernel_objects) |object| {
        assert(kernel_symbol(object).len > 0);
        assert(kernel_meaning(object).len > 0);
        assert(standing_reason(object).len > 0);
        const standing = standing_of(object);
        assert((standing == .held) == (tier_of(object) == .portable));
    }

    // The finding, stated at both ends: the portable tier is held whole, and
    // every object beyond it is owed to the address space.
    assert(held == tier_width(.portable));
    assert(owed == tier_width(.mode) + tier_width(.architecture));
    assert(standing_of(.untyped) == .held);
    assert(standing_of(.notification) == .held);
    assert(standing_of(.giga_page) == .the_address_space);
    assert(standing_of(.page_table) == .the_address_space);

    print("objects: version {s}\n", .{caravan_objects_version});
    print("objects: the kernel offers {d} on riscv64; Caravan stands for {d} and owes {d}.\n", .{ kernel_object_count, held, owed });
    print("objects: three tiers compose with no overlap and no gap -- {d} portable, {d} mode, {d} architecture.\n", .{ tier_width(.portable), tier_width(.mode), tier_width(.architecture) });
    print("objects: the five held are exactly the portable tier -- untyped, TCB, endpoint, notification, capability table.\n", .{});
    print("objects: the four owed are exactly the mode and architecture tiers, and the address space owes every one of them.\n", .{});
    print("objects: {d} further object types stand behind configuration guards and are absent here by choice.\n", .{configuration_guarded_count});
    print("objects: numbering composed, tiers partitioned, agenda honest -- GREEN.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    const allocator = init.arena.allocator();
    const args = try init.minimal.args.toSlice(allocator);

    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "table")) return print_table();
    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "selftest")) return run_self_test();
    if (args.len >= 2) return 1;
    return run_self_test();
}
